\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.10}{\ignorespaces We ran a Monte Carlo simulation of 10,000 iterations with a binary distribution to spread random instances between upper and lower bounds taken from the means of seven year samples. We bounded between 0 and 1 possible data breach events over a year.\endnote { Sample size was set equivalent to the City of Chattanooga to determine the per capita rates for each year over data from  American Samoa and California. The means of those rates were used to set upper and lower bounds with  American Samoa and California representing low and high  cybercrime counts respectively.} \endnote {Monte Carlo Modeling Toolkit authored by Derek E. Brink, CISSP, \url  {www.linkedin.com/in/derekbrink}. March 2023.} }}{92}{figure.caption.39}\protected@file@percent }
